{"_data": [{"contents": [{"content": "", "type": "lesson_name"}, {"content": "Software Development and Engineering: Applet", "type": "unit_name"}, {"content": 1, "type": "position"}, {"content": "stub", "type": "origin"}, {"content": 2, "type": "lesson"}, {"content": "A1.2", "type": "level"}], "type": "metadata"}, {"contents": [{"content": "false", "type": "test"}], "type": "achievment_test"}, {"contents": [{"content": "These are words you use to talk about software development and engineering.", "type": "text"}], "type": "target_word_describer"}, {"contents": [{"content": "Understand key information about the concepts, principles, and methodologies in simple spoken and written situations. Talk about programming techniques, use cases, kinds, and sequences in simple spoken and written situations.", "type": "primary"}, {"content": "Communicates effectively in English about the communicative topics for this level through the use of appropriate basic vocabulary and grammatical structures.  Identifies general aspects related to Software Programming in short written or spoken conversations about past experiences.", "type": "secondary_1"}], "type": "objectives"}, {"content": "enpe7n2le02ob01re1img01.jpg", "type": "background_image"}, {"contents": [{"content": "host program ", "type": "name"}, {"content": "it is any program that supports the applet", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(noun)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: The applet must run in a container that provides a host program through a plug-in.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n2le02ob01aud01", "type": "audio"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"content": "restricted ", "type": "name"}, {"content": "with limits and security", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(adjective)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: Normally,  There is no need of sessions in order to prevent badly or maliciously written applets and the applets are always running within a restricted or \u201csandbox\u201d environment.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n2le02ob01aud02", "type": "audio"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"content": "HTML ", "type": "name"}, {"content": "Hypertext Markup Language. The language for modeling documents and the way in which the information is presented to people", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(noun)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: Applets run in an internet browser and usually in their own window or in a HTML page then its uses are endless.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n2le02ob01aud03", "type": "audio"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"content": "interact ", "type": "name"}, {"content": "to participate with a user through all the options a program has", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(verb)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: Actually, all larger programs are using applets at the moment,  because they provide graphical information and generally interact with the user.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n2le02ob01aud04", "type": "audio"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"content": "useful ", "type": "name"}, {"content": "something that has lots of functionalities and serves different purposes", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(noun)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: The applets are very useful because they run in an internet browser and usually in their own window.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n2le02ob01aud05", "type": "audio"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"content": "container ", "type": "name"}, {"content": "a bigger program in which an applet runs", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(noun)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: The applet must run in a container that provides a host program through a plug-in.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n2le02ob01aud06", "type": "audio"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"content": "Search for information about applet.  ", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Identify the main facts about applet. ", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Write down a paragraph in your own words explaining what an applet is.  ", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Share your paragraph with your class and discuss about the topic.", "type": "text"}], "type": "takeaway_extension"}, {"contents": [{"content": "I search for information about applet. ", "type": "text"}, {"content": "I identify the main facts about applet. ", "type": "text"}, {"content": "I write down a paragraph in my own words explaining what an applet is.  ", "type": "text"}, {"content": "I share information and discuss about the topic.", "type": "text"}], "type": "takeaway_assessment"}, {"contents": [{"content": "<div align=\"center\">Simple Present vs Present Continuous in Affirmative</div>", "type": "heading"}, {"content": "<strong>Simple Present and Present Continuous</strong> tenses are frequently used in English. Both express actions, schedules, arrangements or facts in present, but each one has specific usages and structures.", "type": "describer"}, {"content": "<ul><li>We use the <strong>Simple present tense </strong> to mention daily activities, schedules, habits, general truths, routines and frequent actions. </li> <li>We use the  <strong>present continuous tense</strong> to talk about actions that are happening at the present time and activities that are taking place right now. Take a look at this:</li></ul></br>  <i><b>I study</b> software programming. (It shows a recurrent activity).</i></br></br>  <i><b>I am studying</b> software programming this semester.</i> (It shows that the person is doing the action in this present moment).</br></br>  As you can see both tenses are different in terms of meaning and structure. </br></br>  In the <strong>simple present affirmative</strong> statements with the pronouns he-she-it, you add to the verb <strong>\u2013s ; -es ; -ies</strong> </br></br>  <i>For example: She work<strong>s</strong>; It go<strong>es</strong>; He stud<strong>ies</strong> </i> </br></br>  <strong> The present continuous</strong> statements don't use any auxiliary verbs. Analyze the following chart to understand differences in structure:", "type": "text"}, {"content": "{\"rows\":2,\"columns\":2,\"cells\":[\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<div align=\\\"center\\\">SIMPLE PRESENT</div>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<div align=\\\"center\\\"> PRESENT CONTINUOUS</div>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<strong>Affirmative:</strong></br></br>  Subj + verb + complement\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<strong>Affirmative:</strong></br></br>  Subj + verb to be (present) + main verb (gerund \u2013ing)  + complement\\\"}\"],\"styles\":\" intro_table_border instruction_table_background\"}", "type": "table"}, {"content": "I'm currently studying this topic in my software programming course.   ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "All larger programs are using applets at the moment. ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "The applets are always running within a restricted or \u201csandbox\u201d environment.", "type": "example"}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"content": "<div align=\"center\">The Infinitive Without To after Certain Verbs Let \u2013 Make</div>", "type": "heading"}, {"content": "In English, it is kind of common to use the <span class= \"strong\"> infinitive</span>  without <span class= \"strong\"> to</span>  after modals and certain verbs as <span class= \"strong\"> let, make</span>  and sometimes help.", "type": "describer"}, {"content": "We usually use the verb <strong>let</strong> to make a suggestion.</br></br>  <i>For example:<b> Let's</b> check this new software.</i></br></br>  This verb follows the next structure:</br> <strong>Let + object + bare infinitive (infinitive without 'to')</strong>  </br></br>  We normally use the verb <strong>make</strong> to force someone to do something that he or she doesn't want to do.</br></br>  <i>For example: The teacher always <b>makes</b> us read long articles.</i></br></br>  This verb follows the next structure:</br> <strong>Subject + make + object + bare infinitive (infinitive without 'to')</strong>", "type": "text"}, {"content": "This new game, in this social network, is awesome. Let me show you.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "Well, at college teachers made big efforts to achieve that I understood this theme.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "Let's see. This game is basically done with a software component called applet.", "type": "example"}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"content": "<div align=\"center\">Common Collocations to Talk About Computers</div>", "type": "heading"}, {"content": "<strong>Collocations</strong> are groups of words that usually go together to give meaning as a whole.", "type": "describer"}, {"content": "There are many collocations in English that are often used in everyday language. Some collocations are really strict in its structure since they are always composed of certain words, some other collocations allow the use of synonyms to express the same meaning. </br>  Here are some collocations to talk about computers:</br></br>  <ul><li><strong>Go online:</strong> To connect to a website and have access to it </li> <li><strong>Connect to the internet:</strong> To be ready to start surfing the net.</li> <li><strong>Browse the web:</strong> To check data on the web.</li> <li> <strong>Search engine:</strong> A computer program used to search documents, information and many more things, normally on the World Wide Web.</li></ul>", "type": "text"}], "type": "learning_points"}], "type": "sena", "labs": [{"exercises": [{"questions": [], "template_override": 1, "stimuli_ids": [0], "_data": [{"contents": [{"content": "I can explain what an applet is.  ", "type": "Learning Outcomes"}, {"content": "I can exchange information about applet.  ", "type": "Learning Outcomes"}, {"content": "I can identify 