"}, {"content": "Speaking", "type": "skill"}], "type": "learning_skills"}, {"contents": [{"content": "Instructions: Complete the following steps. Fill in the Self-Assessment.", "type": "text"}], "type": "post_instructions"}], "id": 11, "type": "DWS"}], "_data": [{"contents": [{"content": true, "type": "has_nav"}, {"content": true, "type": "has_exercise_controls"}], "type": "lab_options"}], "stimuli": [{"_data": [], "contents": [{}], "template_override": 1, "id": 0, "type": "welcome"}, {"template_override": 1, "_data": [], "contents": [{"value": {"coverimg": "enpe7n2le02ob01re2img01.jpg", "images": [{"time": "0", "text": "<strong>Kent:</strong> This new game in this social network is awesome. Let me show you. Look, I wonder how these people can create such an excellent game.</br><strong>Sheldon:</strong> Let's see. This game is basically made with a software component called applet. I'm currently studying this topic in my software programming course. An applet is a small program which uses the resources of a larger program such as a web browser. The applet must run in a container that provides a host program through a plug-in. Different from a program, an applet cannot run independently. </br>", "image": "enpe7n2le02ob01re2img01.jpg", "id": 0}, {"time": "34000", "text": "<strong>Kent:</strong> I didn't know that. I guess this is a very popular software then, right?</br><strong>Sheldon:</strong> Yes, pretty much. Actually, all larger programs are using applets at the moment. They are frequently used because they provide graphical information and generally interact with the user.  Normally,  There is no need of sessions in order to prevent badly or maliciously written applets, and the applets are always running within a restricted or \u201csandbox\u201d environment.</br><strong>Kent:</strong> Can you tell me some common examples of applets?</br>", "image": "enpe7n2le02ob01re2img02.jpg", "id": 1}, {"time": "67200", "text": "<strong>Sheldon:</strong> Yes, I can. For example, Java, Flash animations, windows media, and some games designed to be played using web browsers are now utilizing applets. </br><strong>Kent:</strong> Wow, I'm impressed. You know a lot.</br><strong>Sheldon:</strong> Well, at college, teachers made big efforts to achieve that I understood this theme. For instance, the term applet appeared in the early 1990s in connection with Apple's AppleScript for the Macintosh operating system.</br><strong>Kent:</strong> And what can you tell me about features related to applets?</br><strong>Sheldon:</strong> An applet usually performs a very specific function without an independent use. Applets are very useful because they run in an internet browser and usually in their own window. Also, they can be placed in an HTML page then its uses are endless. </br><strong>Kent:</strong> Amazing. Now, I'm thinking on study about that program.</br>", "image": "enpe7n2le02ob01re2img03.jpg", "id": 2}], "audiosrc": "enpe7n2le02ob01re2aud01"}, "type": "slideshow"}], "title": "", "id": 1, "type": "audio"}], "id": 0, "type": "scholar"}]}